 then the situation is even worse. This would be a tactical blunder. This situation is the textbook example of where passing is the most rational play. Let the Landlord make the first move. My teammate's play is designed to gain control. Beating them here is disruptive, wasteful, and counter-productive.\n\nIn the end, it is my evaluation that passing is the only logical choice.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The current situation involves my teammate (Previous Player) playing a high card (♣2) to take control over the Landlord\'s Ace. As a Peasant, my goal is to cooperate. Beating my teammate\'s 2 with my Black Joker would be counter-productive for several reasons:\n1. It wastes my strongest control card (Black Joker) against my own team.\n2. It potentially prevents my teammate from gaining the lead, which they likely fought for by playing the 2.\n3. It relieves pressure on the Landlord. I should force the Landlord to either beat the 2 (forcing out the Red Joker or a Bomb) or pass, giving control to my teammate.\n\nReason: My teammate has played a top-tier card (2) to win the initiative. Interfering by playing the Black Joker would sabotage our team\'s position and waste my best card. The optimal move is to pass and let the Landlord deal with the high card.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
