 information.\n\nIn this context, there is a very high chance I should pass, to let my teammate control the flow. It\'s a key principle in this game: unless I have a bomb or a strong run, I shouldn\'t intervene when my teammate has the lead. My hand is a liability because of its size and scattered strength. Playing any card now could just hand the Landlord an advantage.\n\nThe main strategic imperative here is to maximize my team\'s chance of winning. Given my large, somewhat weak hand and the Landlord acting next, the optimal move is to let my teammate continue their play. If the Landlord wins the trick, I can re-evaluate on the next round. By passing, I put the pressure back on the Landlord.\n\nTherefore, the only correct move is to pass. This allows my teammate to proceed as intended and forces the Landlord to respond to their 8-8. It\'s the most strategically sound decision given the current circumstances.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: My teammate (the Previous Player) has just taken the initiative by beating the Landlord\'s pair of 6s with a pair of 8s. My teammate has only 8 cards remaining, which is a threatening count, while I still have 17 cards. My role in this position (acting just before the Landlord) is usually to let my teammate\'s cards through if they are trying to push, or to play very high cards to force the Landlord. Beating the 8s with 9s is counterproductive as it is a weak block that the Landlord can easily overcome. Beating with Qs or Ks is aggressive and might be useful to force out the Landlord\'s Aces, but given my teammate\'s low card count and initiative, it is statistically better to Pass and let the Landlord deal with the 8s. If the Landlord cannot beat the 8s, my teammate retains control. If the Landlord beats them, I can look for a better opportunity to intervene.\n\nReason: Teammate has initiative and a low hand count (8). Beating their hand would disrupt their strategy. Passing forces the Landlord to respond to the teammate\'s play